  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Calculate the point where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+2x-12\\sqrt{3}y+18=0$ intercepts the $y$-axis.\n\nThe $y$-intercept is $($\n{{field-1}}\n,\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "To calculate the $y$-intercepts, we need to substitute $x=0$ in the equation of the ellipse and try to solve the resulting equation for $y.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}+2x-12\\sqrt{3}y+18 & =0 \\\\ (0)^{2}+6{y}^{2}+2(0)-12\\sqrt{3}y+18 & =0 \\\\ 6{y}^{2}-12\\sqrt{3}y+18 & =0 \\\\ 6[{y}^{2}-2\\sqrt{3}y]+18 & =0 \\\\ 6[(y-\\sqrt{3})^{2}-3]+18 & =0 \\\\ 6(y-\\sqrt{3})^{2}-18+18 & =0 \\\\ 6(y-\\sqrt{3})^{2} & =0 \\\\ y-\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ellipse intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,\\sqrt{3}).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978630/assets/2d833daa50a68c13799763685b4ed961bd3d76d7708b6acb105b9eba6eb39211.png)",
